import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.hw3Av5Vn.js";const n="/llamatron/assets/synth_matrix.BXTtINU7.svg",f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Mod Matrix","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"synths/MOD_MATRIX.md","filePath":"synths/MOD_MATRIX.md"}'),i={name:"synths/MOD_MATRIX.md"};function r(h,e,d,l,c,u){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="mod-matrix" tabindex="-1">Mod Matrix <a class="header-anchor" href="#mod-matrix" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mod Matrix&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The Mod Matrix says what moves what, as the patch cables of a modular synthesizer do, laid out as a table. Its six slots are on the Mod Matrix tab.</p><p><img src="'+n+'" alt="The Mod Matrix tab"></p><p>Each slot has a Source: an envelope, an LFO, an output of a <a href="./LFOS#snail">Snail</a>, the note&#39;s Velocity, or Per Note S&amp;H, a random value drawn as each note starts and held for the whole note, a sample and hold built in; or Osc 1, the note&#39;s own oscillator (Osc 1 to Osc 3 on Asheville, one for each), which moves at audio rate: Osc 1 to Pitch is the bright, clangorous feedback FM of an oscillator modulating itself, Osc 1 to Cutoff the growl of audio-rate filter FM, and Osc 1 to Amp a ring of AM, on any engine, even one that never had FM. An LFO at Very High speed routed to Pitch is the same analog-style FM from a second source (<a href="./LFOS">LFOs</a>). A Via scales the source by a second one, so that, for instance, an LFO moves the cutoff only as far as the note is loud; Per Note S&amp;H is a source only, never a Via. Moves is what it moves, each named by its block, then <code>/</code>, then what it moves: <code>Osc / Pitch</code>, <code>Osc / Pulse Width</code>, <code>Osc / Detune</code> and <code>Osc / Fine</code> (the oscillator&#39;s tuning, by up to a semitone and a tenth of one), <code>Osc / Interval</code> (how far the SID&#39;s second oscillator stands from the note, for sweeping a sync lead), <code>Filter / Cutoff</code>, <code>Filter / Resonance</code>, <code>Amp / Level</code>, or a Modulators slot&#39;s input, such as <code>Mod 1 / Rate</code>. On Asheville, whose three oscillators each have their own, the oscillator&#39;s destinations are each oscillator&#39;s alone, <code>Osc 1 / Pitch</code> to <code>Osc 3 / Fine</code>, so that a route can wobble Osc 2 against the other two. Amount is how far, either way, as a share of what it moves. Mod 1 to <code>Osc / Pitch</code>, with an LFO in Mod 1, is vibrato, with a Square shape and a large Amount a chip trill; Velocity to <code>Filter / Cutoff</code> makes harder notes brighter; Per Note S&amp;H to <code>Osc / Detune</code> makes each note a little different.</p><p>Polarity says how the route reads a source that swings both ways, as an LFO does. Bipolar, the default, moves what it moves from minus the Amount to plus it, as a vibrato bends the pitch either side of the note. Unipolar moves it from nothing to the Amount, so that an LFO on the Cutoff only ever opens the filter from where it is set, never closes it. The Amount shows the range it gives, such as <code>-30% to +30%</code> or <code>0% to +30%</code>; a source that only rises, such as an envelope or Velocity, always moves from nothing to the Amount.</p><h2 id="lfo-rates" tabindex="-1">LFO Rates <a class="header-anchor" href="#lfo-rates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;LFO Rates&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An LFO can speed up and slow down another, the way a player turns a vibrato&#39;s speed knob while holding a note. Moves also offers Mod 1 / Rate to Mod 4 / Rate. A slot whose Source is an LFO moves that LFO&#39;s rate by up to two octaves either way at its full Amount: 50% doubles it at the source&#39;s top and halves it at its bottom. Mod 2, a slow sine, into Mod 1 / Rate makes Mod 1&#39;s vibrato quicken and relax in waves; an LFO into its own rate bends its shape, racing through one half of its cycle and lingering in the other. Unipolar only ever speeds the rate up. A Via scales the route as elsewhere, by another LFO. A Snail&#39;s A and B, <code>Mod 2 / A</code> and <code>Mod 2 / B</code> among the destinations while Mod 2 holds a Snail, take every source (<a href="./LFOS#snail">Snail</a>). The LFOs belong to the whole instrument, not to a note, so only an LFO moves a rate: a slot reading an envelope, Velocity, Per Note S&amp;H, an oscillator or a CC into a rate, or scaling it by one of them, moves nothing. An LFO following the key keeps its ratio to the note and is not moved.</p><h2 id="midi-cc-sources" tabindex="-1">MIDI CC Sources <a class="header-anchor" href="#midi-cc-sources" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;MIDI CC Sources&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A knob on a controller, or a CC row of a pattern, can move the sound through the matrix. The last entry of the Source and Via pickers is <code>MIDI CC</code>. Beside each Source and Via stands a CC field: a dash where the slot reads anything else, and the CC&#39;s number, from 0 to 127, where it reads a MIDI CC, 74 at first. Right moves the cursor onto the number, where <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> step it and ENTER types it. The CC&#39;s value, from 0 to 127, moves its destination from nothing to the slot&#39;s full Amount. A track&#39;s CC rows reach its built-in instrument as they reach any MIDI device, so a pattern whose CC row the track sends as CC 74, with a slot reading CC 74 into Cutoff, opens and closes the filter as it plays, live and in a WAV export alike. A CC starts at 0 until its first message comes.</p><h2 id="how-the-matrix-is-built" tabindex="-1">How the Matrix Is Built <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-matrix-is-built" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the Matrix Is Built&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The routes every synth needs are wired in outside the matrix, as a semi-modular synth&#39;s normalled connections are: Env 1 to the amp, Env 2 to the cutoff through the filter&#39;s Env amount, and the key to the cutoff through Key track. Env 3 is wired to nothing, so that it is free for any use: a route from Env 3 to Pitch gives a pitch envelope. All six slots stay free for movement of your own.</p><p>When an instrument is compiled, empty slots and slots of no amount are left out, and each route keeps its source, its via, its destination and how far it moves at the source&#39;s full value. Each sample, a voice reads only the sources some route uses, multiplies each by its via, and adds what every route gives each destination together, so that two routes to the cutoff add up. Pitch is moved in semitones and the cutoff in octaves, so that a route sounds as wide at a high note as at a low one.</p><h2 id="planned" tabindex="-1">Planned <a class="header-anchor" href="#planned" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Planned&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The agreed design has eight slots, the first two Gate to Env 1 and Gate to Env 2 by default, and each amount shown in its destination&#39;s own unit.</p>',14)])])}const p=t(i,[["render",r]]);export{f as __pageData,p as default};
